# Erdős Problem #99 — low-`t` pentagon activation budget

**Date:** 2026-09-14
**Target:** C-II, canonical full-core Type-II branch
**Dependencies:** low-`t` pentagon sea; empty-pentagon trapezoid endpoint; exact force-hull residual conservation; pentagonal tooth conflict bounds
**Claim class:** proof-only structural refinement awaiting independent audit
**External literature:** no new import

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The current low-`t`, small-residual theorem gives linearly many two-contact pentagonal
teeth whose hidden side is `1+o(1)`. The pointwise `o(1)` statement loses useful
information: the pentagon residual controls the **sum of the activation gaps**.
Keeping that sum produces an `L^1` budget for a simultaneous first-contact attack.

Put

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},\qquad
C_0:=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.00042,
\]

and on a convergent Type-II subsequence write

\[
t:=\lim{q-k\over D},\qquad
\delta:=\lim{b-P_F\over D},\qquad
\kappa:=\lim{k\over D}.
\]

Define

\[
P(t,\delta,\kappa)
:=\pi+C_0-{5t\over2}+\delta+{\kappa\over2}.
\tag{AB.1}
\]

The low-`t` pentagon theorem gives

\[
{p_5\over D}\ge P(t,\delta,\kappa)-o(1).
\tag{AB.2}
\]

Fix any constant

\[
0<c<{2\over3}.
\tag{AB.3}
\]

There is a fixed residual threshold `theta_c>0` such that every pentagonal face `P`
with `r(P)<theta_c` is isolate-free, lies in the flat-trapezoid stability neighborhood,
and has a distinguished flat middle vertex `m` with two hidden diagonals to the two
opposite leaf vertices. If their lengths are `x_P,y_P>1`, then

\[
\boxed{
r(P)\ge c\bigl[(x_P-1)+(y_P-1)\bigr].
}
\tag{AB.4}
\]

Choose from each such pentagon the **shorter** hidden chord and call its excess over
one `g(P)`. Then

\[
\boxed{
g(P)\le {r(P)\over2c}.}
\tag{AB.5}
\]

Consequently, after discarding the at most `R_F/theta_c` high-residual pentagons, one
can choose one two-contact tooth from every remaining pentagon so that

\[
\boxed{
\sum_P g(P)\le {R_F\over2c}.
}
\tag{AB.6}
\]

The standard conflict-graph thinning then gives both

- an endpoint-disjoint hidden-chord family `H_c`, and
- a vertex-disjoint two-contact-tooth family `T_c`,

with

\[
\boxed{
9|H_c|
\ge p_5-{R_F\over\theta_c},
\qquad
13|T_c|
\ge p_5-{R_F\over\theta_c},
}
\tag{AB.7}
\]

and, simultaneously,

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{e\in H_c}(|e|-1)\le {R_F\over2c},
\qquad
\sum_{\tau\in T_c}g(\tau)\le {R_F\over2c}.
}
\tag{AB.8}
\]

For the clean choice `c=3/5`, the total activation budget is

\[
\boxed{{5\over6}R_F.}
\tag{AB.9}
\]

Thus the small-residual branch does not merely contain a linear family of hidden
`1+o(1)` contacts. A linear endpoint-disjoint / vertex-disjoint family can be selected
whose **total radial distance to first contact** is `O(R_F)`.

## 1. Exact area parametrization by the two hidden diagonals

Let an isolate-free convex equilateral pentagonal face be cyclically labeled

\[
l,m,r,a,b
\]

as in the flat-trapezoid endpoint, and set

\[
x:=|m-a|,\qquad y:=|m-b|.
\tag{AB.10}
\]

The boundary sides are all one. Convexity lets the two diagonals from `m` triangulate
the pentagon into triangles with side data

\[
(1,1,x),\qquad (x,1,y),\qquad (y,1,1).
\]

Define

\[
F(s):={s\over4}\sqrt{4-s^2}
\tag{AB.11}
\]

and

\[
G(x,y):={1\over4}
\sqrt{(x+y+1)(-x+y+1)(x-y+1)(x+y-1)}.
\tag{AB.12}
\]

Then exactly

\[
\boxed{A(P)=F(x)+G(x,y)+F(y).}
\tag{AB.13}
\]

For an isolate-free pentagonal exceptional face the canonical residual is

\[
r(P)={A(P)\over a_0}-{3\over2},
\qquad a_0={\sqrt3\over2}.
\tag{AB.14}
\]

At the flat equality trapezoid, `x=y=1` and `r(P)=0`.

## 2. The residual opens each hidden contact linearly

Differentiate (AB.11):

\[
F'(s)={2-s^2\over2\sqrt{4-s^2}},
\qquad F'(1)={1\over2\sqrt3}.
\tag{AB.15}
\]

Also

\[
16G(x,y)^2=4x^2y^2-(x^2+y^2-1)^2,
\]

so

\[
\partial_xG(1,1)=\partial_yG(1,1)={1\over2\sqrt3}.
\tag{AB.16}
\]

Hence, writing the right side of (AB.14) as `rho(x,y)`,

\[
\boxed{
\partial_x\rho(1,1)=\partial_y\rho(1,1)={2\over3}.
}
\tag{AB.17}
\]

For any fixed `c<2/3`, continuity gives `eta_c>0` such that both partial derivatives
are at least `c` throughout `1<=x,y<=1+eta_c`. Integrating first in one coordinate
and then the other gives

\[
\rho(x,y)\ge c[(x-1)+(y-1)].
\]

Tighten the pre-existing trapezoid/Wegner stability threshold so every retained
pentagon lies in this rectangle. This proves (AB.4).

Triangle-freeness gives `x,y>1`: equality for `x`, for example, would make the three
unit edges `mr`, `ra`, `am` an exact unit equilateral triangle.

## 3. Choosing the nearer tooth improves the global constant

Let

\[
g(P):=\min\{x_P-1,y_P-1\}.
\]

From (AB.4),

\[
g(P)
\le{(x_P-1)+(y_P-1)\over2}
\le {r(P)\over2c}.
\tag{AB.18}
\]

Choose the leaf tooth corresponding to this shorter hidden chord. The exceptional-face
residuals are nonnegative and sum to at most `R_F`, so over every retained family

\[
\sum_Pg(P)
\le {1\over2c}\sum_Pr(P)
\le {R_F\over2c}.
\tag{AB.19}
\]

This is strictly stronger than summing both hidden gaps. In particular `c=3/5`
gives the fixed safe coefficient `5/6` in (AB.9). Letting `c` approach `2/3` shows
that the local endpoint coefficient can be made arbitrarily close to `3/4`, at the
cost of shrinking the fixed stability neighborhood.

## 4. Disjoint extraction preserves the `L^1` budget

After deleting all pentagons with residual at least `theta_c`, at least

\[
p_5-{R_F\over\theta_c}
\tag{AB.20}
\]

good pentagons remain. Choose the nearer tooth from each one as in Section 3.

For hidden chords, a source endpoint lies on at most five selected bounded faces, so a
chosen chord conflicts with at most eight others. Greedy thinning keeps at least one
ninth of the family. For whole three-vertex teeth, the conflict degree is at most
`3(5-1)=12`, so greedy thinning keeps at least one thirteenth. This proves (AB.7).

All activation gaps are nonnegative. Passing to either thinned subfamily can only
decrease their sum, so (AB.19) immediately gives (AB.8).

## 5. Low-`t`, small-residual consequences

If

\[
R_F=o(D),
\tag{AB.21}
\]

then `R_F/theta_c=o(D)` for every fixed `c<2/3`. Combining (AB.2), (AB.7), and
(AB.8) gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf{|H_c|\over D}\ge {P(t,\delta,\kappa)\over9},
\qquad
\liminf{|T_c|\over D}\ge {P(t,\delta,\kappa)\over13},
}
\tag{AB.22}
\]

while the total activation gap of each family is at most `R_F/(2c)=o(D)`.

At the current proof-only floor

\[
t_{42}={4\over9}\left(\pi+\sqrt{\Delta_2}\right)+0.0013751
=1.8208886379794447\ldots,
\]

and at the most permissive `delta=kappa=0`,

\[
P(t_{42},0,0)=0.4944166723690961\ldots .
\tag{AB.23}
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
{|H_{3/5}|\over D}\ge0.0549351858187884\ldots-o(1),
}
\tag{AB.24}
\]

\[
\boxed{
{|T_{3/5}|\over D}\ge0.0380320517206997\ldots-o(1),
}
\tag{AB.25}
\]

and each family's total hidden-side excess is at most

\[
\boxed{{5\over6}R_F.}
\tag{AB.26}
\]

The same `t<=1.8585` low-`t` band gives

\[
{|H_{3/5}|\over D}\ge0.0444875852575231\ldots-o(1),
\qquad
{|T_{3/5}|\over D}\ge0.0307990974859775\ldots-o(1),
\tag{AB.27}
\]

again with total activation gap at most `5R_F/6`.

## 6. Frontier effect

The hard low-`t`, small-residual branch now contains a macroscopic disjoint family for
which the **aggregate motion needed to hit hidden unit contacts is sublinear**:

```text
~0.055 D endpoint-disjoint hidden chords (near the t42 floor)
+ total hidden-side excess <= (5/6) R_F = o(D),
```

and simultaneously a vertex-disjoint three-source tooth family of density
`~0.038D` with the same aggregate bound.

This removes a bookkeeping obstacle from a simultaneous deformation argument. A
closing theorem no longer needs a uniform lower bound on every hidden gap; it would be
enough to construct a source-preserving feasible motion with aggregate available
inward displacement larger than this `L^1` budget.

No such global motion is proved here. The theorem does not assign KKT weight to the
hidden chords, and near contacts are not promoted to exact contacts by compactness.
C-II and Level I remain open.

## 7. Scope audit

- The area identity is exact for the isolate-free convex equilateral pentagon normal
  form.
- The derivative argument is local but rigorous: only continuity at `(1,1)` is used.
- `theta_c` is a fixed positive threshold after intersecting the derivative,
  trapezoid-stability, and first Wegner-jump neighborhoods.
- The low-`t` count uses actual pentagons, so no second `Q-f` payment occurs.
- The `R_F=o(D)` hypothesis is needed only for the asymptotic cardinality statements;
  (AB.4)--(AB.8) are finite residual-budget statements after the fixed threshold is
  chosen.
- All hidden chords are inactive actual source pairs of length strictly greater than
  one.
- No exact triangle and no same-cardinality strict sub-`D` competitor is claimed.
